        "point": "LT only",
        "statement": "The predicate requires only a bare `LT` instance on the block-index type α; no transitivity, reflexivity, or linearity is assumed for the predicate itself.",
        "note": "Stronger order assumptions (e.g., LinearOrder) appear only in derived theorems."
      },
      {
        "point": "Same-block entries unconstrained",
        "statement": "When `b i = b j` the condition `b j < b i` is false, so entries within the same diagonal block are subject to no constraint whatsoever.",
        "note": "This is a direct consequence of the strict inequality in the definition."
      },
      {
        "point": "Constant b",
        "statement": "If `b` is constant, the predicate is vacuously true for every matrix, treating the whole matrix as one block.",
        "note": "No junk value; the predicate is simply true in this degenerate case."
      }
    ]
  },
